## [Chapter 1: Data visualization](https://r4ds.hadley.nz/data-visualize)

### 1.2.2 - 1.2.4

<!-- Plot 1 -->
```{r}
ggplot(
  data = penguins,
  mapping = aes(x = flipper_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)
) +
  geom_point(aes(color = species, shape = species)) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm") +
  labs(
    title = "Body mass and flipper length",
    subtitle = "Dimensions for Adelie, Chinstrap, and Gentoo Penguins",
    x = "Flipper length (mm)", y = "Body mass (g)",
    color = "Species", shape = "Species"
  ) +
  scale_color_colorblind()
```

### 1.4.3

<!-- Plot 2 & 3: -->
```{r}
ggplot(penguins, aes(x = species)) +
  geom_bar(color = "red")

ggplot(penguins, aes(x = species)) +
  geom_bar(fill = "red")
```

### 1.5.1 

Example: side-by-side box plots.
```{r}
ggplot(penguins, aes(x = species, y = body_mass_g)) +
  geom_boxplot()
```

Example: overlayed density plots
```{r}
ggplot(penguins, aes(x = body_mass_g, color = species, fill = species)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.5)
```

### 1.5.2

**Stacked Bar Charts**

Frequency Plot:
```{r}
ggplot(penguins, aes(x = island, fill = species)) +
  geom_bar()
```

Relative Frequency Plot:
```{r}
ggplot(penguins, aes(x = island, fill = species)) +
  geom_bar(position = "fill") +
  labs(y = "proportion")
```

### 1.5.4

To plot three of more variables we can map additional variables to different aesthetics such as color (species) and shape (island).
```{r}
ggplot(penguins, aes(x = flipper_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)) +
  geom_point(aes(color = species, shape = island))
```

Or use facets:
```{r}
ggplot(penguins, aes(x = flipper_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)) +
  geom_point(aes(color = species, shape = species)) +
  facet_wrap(~island)
```


## Data and Visualization Summary

**A numerical and a categorical variable:** 

* Side-by-Side Box Plot
* Histogram
* Density Curves

**Two categorical variables:**

* Stacked Bar Chart:
    a. Frequency Plot
    b. Relative Frequency Plot

**Two numerical variables:**

* Scatterplot

**Three of more variables:**

* Scatterplot + mapping additional variables to different aesthetics (shape, color, etc.)
* Faceting your plots





