## Plotting Large Datasets

```{r, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, echo=FALSE}
library(ggbeeswarm)
```

Let's look the count of flights by carrier.
```{r}
ggplot(data = flights) +
  aes(x= fct_infreq(carrier)) +
  geom_bar()
```

Let's look at departure delay.
```{r}
ggplot(data = flights,
       aes(x = dep_delay)) +
  geom_histogram(color = "white")
```

Apply a square root transform to see where the bulk of the data sits.
```{r}
ggplot(data = flights,
       aes(x = dep_delay)) +
  geom_histogram(color = "white") +
  scale_x_continuous(trans = "sqrt")
  #scale_x_sqrt() #ROTATE  #scale_x_sqrt()
```

Zooming in on the data using scale limits is not preferred as it eliminates data from possible future calculations.
```{r}
ggplot(data = flights,
       aes(x = dep_delay)) +
  geom_histogram() +
  scale_x_continuous(
    limits = c(-50, 250))
```

The preferred approach is adjust the coordinate layer.
```{r}
ggplot(data = flights,
       aes(x = dep_delay)) +
  geom_histogram(
    color = "white",
    binwidth = 10
                 ) +
  coord_cartesian(
    xlim = c(-25, 250))
```

Let's build a boxplot.
```{r}
ggplot(data = flights) +
  aes(x = fct_reorder(carrier, dep_delay, na.rm = TRUE)) +
  aes(y = dep_delay) +
geom_boxplot()
```

Let's try zooming in again but this time on a boxplot when the boxplots might be squished as above.
```{r}
ggplot(data = flights) +
  aes(x = fct_reorder(carrier, dep_delay, na.rm = TRUE)) +
  aes(y = dep_delay) +
 geom_boxplot() +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(-50,250))
```

Or we can do a violin plot.
```{r}
ggplot(data = flights,
       aes(x = fct_reorder(carrier, dep_delay),
           y = dep_delay)) +
  geom_violin() +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(-50, 250))
```

Using the Iris dataset let's compare the boxplot, violin plot, and the bee swarm plot.
```{r}
# Base Plot
p <- ggplot(data = iris, aes(x = Species, y = Petal.Length))

# Boxplot
p +
  geom_boxplot()  

# Violin Plot
p +
  geom_violin()
  
# Bee Swarm Plot
p +
  geom_beeswarm()

# Quasi-random Plot
p +
  geom_quasirandom()
```

Let's do the same thing but with a reduced set of the Flights dataset.
```{r}
p <- flights %>% sample_n(size=1000) %>%
      ggplot(aes(x = carrier,y = dep_delay))

# Boxplot
p +
  geom_boxplot() +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(-50, 500))

# Violin Plot 
p +
  geom_violin() +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(-50, 500))
  
# Bee Swarm Plot
p +
  geom_beeswarm(cex = .15) +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(-50, 500))

# Quasi-random Plot
p +
  geom_quasirandom() +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(-50, 500))
```





